7418	{"artifactId":"LLD-0764c4bcaa076f8e-s1","entry":{"id":"review-6120e6ec3d8fc89c2e2bbdd221dc5121","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-29T10:59:10.834Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"pass","summary":"review: pass","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"pass","findings":[{"claimId":"fix-targets-defect.1","ref":"fix-targets-defect","item":"fix-targets-defect","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The LLD's withPublicationFence signature and purpose match exactly the approved AMD-c32aa60534232f36-1 sharedContract.methodAdd for sc4 (same type params, same freeze/takeover/fence/null-retry semantics), and the scope split (sc4=recovery/fence only, S5=canonical coverage/CR, S6=rollout) matches the ISSUE's fixIntent verbatim.","evidence":"AMD-c32aa60534232f36-1.json body.amendment.method.signature is identical to the LLD's 2.1 signature; AMD rationale ('Current s4 cannot reacquire completion-requested runs and cannot durably bind a publication intent') matches ISSUE-0764c4bcaa076f8e.json body.rootCause/fixIntent, and both explicitly keep coverage/CR in S5 and rollout in S6. [files: .insrc/artifacts/AMD-c32aa60534232f36-1.json, .insrc/artifacts/ISSUE-0764c4bcaa076f8e.json]","action":"none — consistent","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"foldRecoveryLifecycle currently rejects a lease claim on a run whose state is 'completion-requested', grouping it with the terminal states ('completed','cancelled','stale').","evidence":"recovery-lifecycle.ts:21 — `if(['completed','cancelled','stale','completion-requested'].includes(old.state))throw new RecoveryError('corrupt','Cannot claim terminal/completion run.');` [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-lifecycle.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The current RecoveryView (and its ReviewRecoveryV1 projection) has no field recording a durably frozen publication intent/checkpoint binding, so there is nothing today to recover a publication from.","evidence":"recovery-replay.ts:13-18 defines RecoveryView with accounting/manifest/basis/state/receipts/outstandingWork/eventOrdinal/eventDigest/lifecycle only — no publication field; recoveryProjection in recovery-lifecycle.ts:38-42 likewise builds ReviewRecoveryV1 with no publication/intent field. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-replay.ts, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-lifecycle.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"All five files the design names as change sites (recovery-store.ts, recovery-lifecycle.ts, recovery-types.ts, recovery-replay.ts, recovery-journal.ts) exist and already contain the exact hooks the design proposes to extend: RecoveryStore class + journal.exclusive calls, foldRecoveryLifecycle's event-kind dispatch, the RecoveryEventKind union, the RecoveryView interface, and the per-canonical-root queue (`queues` Map) in RecoveryJournal.","evidence":"Read each file in full; e.g. recovery-journal.ts:15-17 `const queues = new Map<string, RootQueue>();` / `this.queue = queues.get(this.directory) ?? ...` implements exactly the 'existing canonical run-root queue' the design (3.4) says it enters, and RecoveryStore.claim (recovery-store.ts:43-47) is the exact call site the ISSUE's reproduction names. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-store.ts, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-journal.ts, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-lifecycle.ts, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-replay.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"preserved-behaviour.1","ref":"preserved-behaviour","item":"preserved-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design's claim that no change touches the approved ReviewRecoveryV1 public shape or canonical artifact JSON is consistent with the actual projection function, which the design does not need to modify for sc4's own fencing state.","evidence":"recoveryProjection (recovery-lifecycle.ts:38-42) builds the public ReviewRecoveryV1 object field-by-field from view.manifest/lifecycle/accounting/state only; the design's new `view.lifecycle` publication-adjacent data is placed on the private RecoveryView (3.1), not threaded into this function, so the public shape is untouched by the plan as written. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-lifecycle.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"preserved-behaviour.2","ref":"preserved-behaviour","item":"preserved-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The test infrastructure the design's test strategy depends on (deterministic recoveryFixture, a fork-based child-process crash worker, fault-injection points) already exists, so 'extend the existing crash harness' is a real, not invented, foundation for proving the preserved invariants under crash/concurrency.","evidence":"__tests__/recovery-fixtures.ts and __tests__/recovery-integration.test.ts exist; the latter uses `fork(new URL('./recovery-crash-worker.ts', ...))` with `stdio:[...,'ipc']` to simulate a real process crash and resume, matching the design's 'extend recovery child worker with publication fault boundaries' fixture claim. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/__tests__/recovery-integration.test.ts, src/workflow/code-review/investigation/__tests__/recovery-fixtures.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The acceptance-mapping table's four criteria (ac1 durable intent before callback / idempotent replay / conflict refusal; ac2 takeover preserves frozen state; ac3 timeout fences without false exactly-once; ac4 crash/lost-ACK reconciliation and legacy-state refusal) each correspond to a concrete, checkable behavior change (the new journal event kind, the modified claim-eligibility check, and the new fenced-callback method) rather than to prose with no testable surface.","evidence":"Each ac maps 1:1 onto one of the three data-model changes (3.2 journal transition, 3.3 takeover invariant-change, 3.4 bounded fence) and their corresponding error-path rows in section 5, and the named fixtures (gated callback/fake timer, forged/corrupt intent values, extended crash worker) are concrete test mechanisms that exist in this test suite's established pattern, not aspirational tooling. [files: src/workflow/code-review/investigation/__tests__/recovery-integration.test.ts]","action":"none — no test code exists yet since this is an LLD, not a build; this premise checks only that the design names a crisp mapping and real fixtures, not that tests currently pass","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":0,"low":7,"unverified":0},"template":"design-issue","reviewedAt":"2026-10-29T10:59:10.834Z","model":"cli-claude:claude-sonnet-5","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
